;;; A wildcard `_` in an hrule's `:match` proposes nothing — **W20**,
;;; register row `W20/wild-in-hrule` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), the
;;; case's mechanism in an hrule.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(holds a b)`, and one hrule that proposes `(tag ?x)` for each
;;; `holds` fact, whatever its second argument: the pattern writes `_` there.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A bare `_` is reserved and refused wherever it is written (Q-M1e2.22):
;;; the program is refused at the parse, at the `_`. With `_` written `?any`
;;; (`wild-in-hrule-twin.ein`) the hrule proposes `(tag a)`, H is that one
;;; candidate, nothing refutes, and R is `∅` and `{(tag a)}`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and what it answers ──────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `c73a0796`, in a release build and a debug one, H was empty: `_` was
;;; an opaque slot that matched nothing, so the hrule matched no fact and
;;; proposed no candidate. `ein solve FILE` printed *no candidate: R is {∅}
;;; or empty, decided by root alone*, and R was `{∅}`. Today the parse
;;; refuses the `_`.
;;;

(relation holds Node Node) (relation tag Node)
(holds a b)
(hrule h () :match (holds ?x _) :assert (tag ?x))
